it is a crunch week in the brexit  process  all eyes are on whether a trade deal can  be struck  both sides know it is now or never  britain's chief negotiator david frost  determined not to be distracted by the  comings and goings in downing street  but rather set on trying to get that  trade deal  next week are you staying in posts  now in brussels for talks he tweeted we  are working to get  a deal but the only one that's possible  is one that is compatible with our  sovereignty  and takes back control of our laws our  trade and our waters  that has been our consistent position  from the start  and i will not be changing it he wrote  one sticking point threatens to bring  things to a standstill  and that's the uk's insistence on a kind  of trade insurance policy  which breaks the terms of the withdrawal  agreement signed by the prime minister  back in january  i've spoken to a lot of eu capitals  about this and  also eu commissioners and i think there  is an absolutely  unanimous view that if the british  government deliberately  decides to break international law and  undermine a treaty that's not even 12  months old  well then why would the eu sign up to a  new deal um  when the uk is breaking the existing one  i hope you were listening to  good morning simon coveney interview um  he said that the eu won't do  a trade deal there's unanimous agreement  on that if the uk government tries to  restore measures to the internal market  bill which break  the law are you going to try and do that  yes we will be putting those measures  back because they are very important  they just really  bring clarity to mechanisms that are  already  in the withdrawal agreement this is all  about the internal market bill  which the government brought in they say  to ensure the trade of goods and  services  between all four home nations remain  barrier-free  after the brexit transition period ends  at the end of the year  one of the major issues is how the same  rules can apply to northern  ireland when it shares a border with the  republic of ireland  which will remain in the eu the eu says  the bill  breaks the terms of the withdrawal  agreement as well as international law  the bill ended up here at the house of  lords and pairs voted overwhelmingly to  take out the bits  that would allow ministers to break  international law  but the government is now determined to  put those bits  straight back in again and that sets the  tone  for the final stages of britain's  negotiation with europe  i think the difficult thing for the  prime minister is that any deal that is  reached with the eu  will still come with quite a lot of  disruption and so if he does  make compromises and and agree something  that still makes it much harder to trade  with the eu the prime minister will then  need to own that  whereas in a no deal scenario it's sort  of easier to blame the eu for any  disruption that's coming and i think  that's ultimately what the prime  minister is going to have to to make a  decision on  it's an impasse a stalemate the kind of  stubborn  deadlock that has defined brexit from  the very start  mick martin sky news westminster
 

